          ],
          "html_parent_source_id": null
        },
        {
          "source_id": "worthington-1872-howell-v1",
          "section": "p140-crary",
          "locator": "p140-crary",
          "quote": "Lieutenant George F. Crary, 46th Ohio, sworn:\n\"Question by prisoner. Did you see me on Saturday morning out with the pickets?\n\"A. I saw you out there early in the morning. We were driven away about 7 a. m., (Sept. 5, 1862.) I heard a rebel drum beat Friday afternoon. The beat appeared to be in more than one regiment.\"",
          "raw_sha256": "d788db7f16a74cfacbdeaeb5ba11aba6b151aa72d4fbe9d21e9110d9632c029b",
          "source_metadata_sha256": "9088c2677b13e9575855af524e18028d4fe679da170c7382fae2bea7d2eb8ec3",
          "document_date": null,
          "historical_knowledge_at": null,
          "visually_checked_facsimile_ids": [
            "worthington-howell-p140-facsimile-v1"
          ],
          "html_parent_source_id": null
        },
        {
          "source_id": "worthington-1872-howell-v1",
          "section": "p137-sherman-denial",
          "locator": "p137-sherman-denial",
          "quote": "He testified \"that no pickets were driven out of the Howell house on Saturday. The house,\" he says, \"which I call the Widow Howell's house, was in a field, near a lane, in Buckland's front, about three-quarters of a mile from his center. Our pickets were a mile in front of that house.",
          "raw_sha256": "d788db7f16a74cfacbdeaeb5ba11aba6b151aa72d4fbe9d21e9110d9632c029b",
          "source_metadata_sha256": "9088c2677b13e9575855af524e18028d4fe679da170c7382fae2bea7d2eb8ec3",
